(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-premalignant-and-malignant-lesions-quiz"]={"title":"Pre-Malignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why must oedema severity not be used to judge Kaposi sarcoma burden?","choices":["Oedema resolves within days of starting treatment","Marked oedema may occur with few or no visible skin lesions","Oedema in Kaposi sarcoma is always caused by the antiretroviral therapy itself","Oedema only appears once visceral disease is established"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck says explicitly not to use oedema severity alone. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What does the deck say patients should be told about establishing the cutaneous T-cell lymphoma diagnosis?","choices":["Blood testing establishes the diagnosis before any biopsy","It may require repeated biopsies, and the disease is usually chronic","A single biopsy is definitive in nearly all cases, and the disease is self-limiting","The diagnosis is made clinically and biopsy is unnecessary"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — setting that expectation early matters, because the diagnosis is often slow to confirm. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What self-examination does the deck ask melanoma patients to perform, and which sites must be included?","choices":["Annual self-examination using the recognition mnemonic alone, limited to the sun-exposed skin of the face, arms and upper chest","Monthly self-examination of the trunk and limbs only, since acral, nail and scalp melanoma are too rare to warrant routine checking","No self-examination at all, since surveillance of the skin, scalp, nails and acral sites is entirely the specialist team's responsibility","Monthly self-examination using the recognition mnemonic and ugly-duckling principles, including the scalp, back, palms, soles and nails"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the acral and scalp sites are easy to miss. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"In the TNM melanoma staging overview, what do the three letters denote?","choices":["T is the time since onset; N is the number of tumour-involved regional lymph nodes; M is the number of distant metastases","T is primary tumour thickness; N is the nodal diameter in centimetres; M is the mitotic rate","T is primary tumour thickness; N is the number of tumour-involved regional lymph nodes; M is the number of metastases at a distant site","T is the tumour's anatomic level; N is the number of naevi present; M is the number of mitoses per high-power field"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the footnote of the staging table defines all three. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"In the Stages of Melanoma diagram, what distinguishes stage III from stage IV?","choices":["Stage III is melanoma confined to the epidermis; stage IV is localised disease in the skin","Stage III is spread to the lymph nodes; stage IV is spread to other organs","Stage III is spread to other organs; stage IV is spread to the lymph nodes","Stage III is localised disease thicker than stage II; stage IV is spread to the lymph nodes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — nodal against distant spread. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"How often should a patient treated for squamous cell carcinoma be examined, and how does immunosuppression change it?","choices":["At least an annual skin examination alone, at an interval unchanged by immunosuppression","Every three months for life in all patients","Only when the patient reports a new lesion","At least annual skin and lymph-node examination, with closer intervals if high-risk or immunosuppressed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the interval tightens rather than the examination changing. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What does the deck say about imaging in typical localized basal cell carcinoma?","choices":["Magnetic resonance imaging is required whenever the lesion is anywhere on the face or scalp","Imaging replaces biopsy when the lesion is in a tissue-sensitive site","Routine imaging is not needed; it is reserved for advanced, deeply invasive or metastatic disease","Routine imaging of the affected region is required before any excision"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and that specialist-directed workup is the exception rather than the rule. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What increases recurrence and functional morbidity in nail unit squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Photographing the lesion before referral","A negative human papillomavirus result","Incomplete excision, immunosuppression, delayed diagnosis and bone invasion","Complete margin-controlled surgery with clear histologic margins"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is often curable with complete surgery, which is why incompleteness matters. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"Which lesion features make early squamous cell carcinoma the distinction to exclude in an actinic keratosis?","choices":["Rough, flat, flesh-coloured, and more apparent by touch than it is by sight","Pearly, translucent, with telangiectasias","Scaly, symmetric and under two millimetres","Thick, indurated, ulcerated, enlarging, painful, bleeding, persistent or recurrent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the deck's full list of features that shift the concern to carcinoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which Kaposi sarcoma form may REGRESS, and what makes it do so?","choices":["Endemic disease, which regresses with antiretroviral therapy","Epidemic disease, which regresses once chemotherapy stops","Iatrogenic disease, which may regress when immunosuppression is reduced","Classic disease, which regresses spontaneously after a course of local radiation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — which is why the transplant team is consulted before any reduction. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which examination priorities does the deck list for a suspected malignant cutaneous lesion?","choices":["Dermoscopy alone, since the total-body survey, the palms, soles and nails, the oral mucosa and the regional nodes all belong to the dermatologist rather than the referring clinician","Good lighting, dermoscopy when trained, and a total-body skin survey when indicated; palms, soles and nails; oral mucosa when Kaposi sarcoma is possible; regional nodes for invasive or high-risk disease","Good lighting and dermoscopy in every patient regardless of training; palms, soles and nails; oral mucosa in every patient; a total-body survey reserved for those who already have a skin cancer diagnosis","Wood lamp examination and potassium hydroxide preparation of any scale; palms, soles and nails; oral mucosa when tinea is possible; regional nodes in every patient regardless of risk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — note the oral mucosa is tied specifically to Kaposi sarcoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"The deck says to photograph a concerning pigmented band. What caution comes with that?","choices":["Do not photograph it at all, because nail pigment renders inaccurately on a phone camera","Do not photograph it until the polish has regrown","Do not photograph more than one nail at a time","Do not let photographing it delay referral"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — documentation is for tracking change, not for postponing the specialist. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"What defines stage IV melanoma in the staging overview?","choices":["Any T, any N, with M1 — that is, distant metastasis present whatever the primary or nodal status","T4b, N3a/b/c, with M0 — the deepest primary with extensive nodal disease but no distant spread","Any T with N0 and M0 — a thick primary with no nodal or distant disease","Tis with any N and any M — in situ disease with any degree of spread"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — M1 alone determines it. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which basal cell carcinoma subtype list determines behaviour and treatment?","choices":["The HISTOLOGIC list: superficial, nodular, micronodular and infiltrative","The CLINICAL list: superficial, nodular, pigmented and morpheaform subtypes","Either list, since the two are identical","Neither, since size determines treatment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and note it differs from the CLINICAL list, which is superficial, nodular, pigmented and morpheaform. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which team must be involved BEFORE immunosuppression is reduced in iatrogenic Kaposi sarcoma?","choices":["Palliative care","Pulmonology or gastroenterology","The transplant team","Medical oncology, who direct all systemic and immunosuppressive decisions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the deck lists them explicitly as a referral before reducing immunosuppression. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"What must the pathologist distinguish when an actinic keratosis is biopsied, and why does it matter?","choices":["Whether the lesion sits on a field-cancerized site, because that guides how often the patient has to be brought back for surveillance review","Actinic keratosis versus squamous cell carcinoma in situ versus invasive squamous cell carcinoma, because invasion changes treatment, margins and risk substantially","Actinic keratosis versus seborrhoeic keratosis, because the treatments differ","Hypertrophic versus atrophic actinic keratosis, because that guides field therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — three possibilities, not two, and the consequence is what makes it worth asking. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is Kaposi sarcoma immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome?","choices":["Kaposi sarcoma present when antiretroviral therapy begins can worsen as the immune system recovers","Kaposi sarcoma appearing for the first time several years after antiretroviral therapy begins","Kaposi sarcoma that fails to respond to antiretroviral therapy at all","An allergic reaction to liposomal doxorubicin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the treatment itself can make the disease worse before it improves. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which melanoma subtype is described as rapidly growing, often amelanotic, and may LACK the classic recognition features?","choices":["Acral lentiginous melanoma","Nodular melanoma","Superficial spreading melanoma","Lentigo maligna melanoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it may lack ABCDE features, which makes it high-risk. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which features does the deck say to use when discriminating Kaposi sarcoma from its lookalikes?","choices":["Chronicity, treatment resistance, follicular hair loss and scarring alopecia of the scalp","Immune status, distribution, oral lesions, oedema, visceral symptoms and biopsy — not colour alone","Colour, size and symmetry of the lesions","Breslow thickness, ulceration and mitotic rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the warning against relying on colour is the point of the list. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which basal cell carcinoma subtype is scar-like and carries the highest risk of subclinical spread?","choices":["Morpheaform or sclerosing — a scar-like or ivory-white lesion whose extension beyond the visible pink segment is clinically subtle","Superficial — a reddish shiny scaly thin plaque whose extension beyond the visible segment is clinically subtle","Nodular — a pearly papule with central erosion whose extension beyond the visible segment is clinically subtle","Pigmented — a stippled lesion whose extension beyond the visible segment is clinically subtle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the invisible extension is what makes it high-risk. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"How is a suspected squamous cell carcinoma diagnosed, and what must the sample achieve?","choices":["Shave biopsy only, since deeper sampling is thought to risk seeding the tumour into the dermis","Clinical diagnosis alone, with biopsy reserved for recurrence","Dermoscopy, with biopsy only if dermoscopy is inconclusive","Shave, punch or excisional biopsy sampling sufficient depth to separate in situ from invasive disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — depth is the point, because in situ and invasive carry different treatment, margins and risk. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What does the deck say about what sentinel lymph node biopsy achieves?","choices":["It is a screening procedure used before any biopsy of the primary lesion","It replaces re-excision of the primary lesion when nodes are negative","It is a STAGING procedure, and may not itself improve overall survival","It is a THERAPEUTIC procedure that reliably improves overall survival"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — an important expectation to set with a patient. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"How does the deck define the immune context once Kaposi sarcoma is suspected?","choices":["Test for human immunodeficiency virus if status is unknown; if known, assess CD4 count, viral load, antiretroviral history and adherence","Test for human immunodeficiency virus only if the patient reports risk factors on history, and otherwise assume seronegativity","Assess CD4 count alone, since it determines prognosis","No immune assessment is needed once biopsy confirms the diagnosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — knowing the status is not enough, the degree of immune suppression matters. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Describe the actinic keratosis lesion, including the finding the deck says may be more apparent by touch than by sight.","choices":["Small violaceous or purple macules and plaques, flesh-coloured early on, that are softer than surrounding skin and more apparent by sight than by touch","Small 0.2 to 0.6 cm flesh-coloured, pink or slightly hyperpigmented papules with a characteristic sandpaper texture on palpation","Large 2 to 6 cm deeply pigmented plaques, flesh-coloured at the edge, with a smooth waxy stuck-on surface that is more apparent by sight than by touch","Small 0.2 to 0.6 cm pearly translucent or pink papules with visible telangiectasias that become more apparent when the skin is stretched"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the sandpaper texture is often felt before it is seen. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which features are NOT part of a typical actinic keratosis and should prompt biopsy consideration?","choices":["Bleeding, induration, ulceration or rapid enlargement","Tenderness on palpation and a rough surface texture","Flesh-coloured or pink colour and a size under a centimetre","Location on the dorsal hand and the presence of surrounding sun damage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — none of these belong to a typical lesion. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which blood tests does the deck list for ADVANCED cutaneous T-cell lymphoma?","choices":["Complete blood count with differential, eosinophilia, circulating Sezary cells, T-cell gene-rearrangement testing and specialist flow cytometry","Complete blood count alone, repeated at every single visit until the disease is judged to be stable by the treating specialist","Human herpesvirus 8 serology and CD4 count","Serum lead level and ferritin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — these belong to advanced disease rather than to the initial skin diagnosis. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A biopsy for suspected cutaneous T-cell lymphoma comes back nondiagnostic, but the clinical picture still fits. What does the deck say?","choices":["The diagnosis is excluded and an alternative should be pursued","Proceed directly to systemic therapy on clinical grounds alone","Repeat the biopsy from the same site after treating it topically for two full weeks first","A single nondiagnostic biopsy does not exclude the disease; correlate clinically and reassess over time"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — and numerous biopsies may be needed, which is why patients are warned in advance. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What is preferred for invasive squamous cell carcinoma, and what is used for advanced or metastatic disease?","choices":["Surgical excision or Mohs micrographic surgery for invasive disease; programmed death 1 blockade and cetuximab for advanced or metastatic disease","Imiquimod or topical fluorouracil for invasive disease; surgical excision for advanced or metastatic disease","Surgical excision or Mohs micrographic surgery for invasive disease; hedgehog pathway inhibitors for advanced or metastatic disease","Radiation therapy alone for invasive disease; curettage and electrodesiccation for advanced or metastatic disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — advanced disease needs specialist multidisciplinary care. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which melanoma subtype does the deck say is frequently delayed in diagnosis, making early reporting critical?","choices":["Superficial spreading melanoma, the commonest subtype overall","Lentigo maligna melanoma","Nodular melanoma","Acral lentiginous melanoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — which is why patients are told to report new pigment, widening bands or periungual pigment promptly. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Slide 50's diagram maps levels I to V against skin anatomy (this is the system conventionally called the Clark level). Which layers do they correspond to?","choices":["I into the papillary dermis; II into the reticular dermis; III into subcutaneous tissue; IV into muscle; V into bone","I confined to the stratum corneum; II into the epidermis; III into the papillary dermis; IV into the reticular dermis; V into the dermis-subcutaneous junction","I confined to the epidermis; II into the papillary dermis; III filling the papillary dermis; IV into the reticular dermis; V into the subcutaneous tissue","I confined to the epidermis; II into the reticular dermis; III filling the reticular dermis; IV into the papillary dermis; V into the subcutaneous tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the level of invasion is an ANATOMIC layer, unlike Breslow thickness which is a measurement. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 50"}]};
